> +  useEffect(() => {
+    setCurrentPage(1);
+  }, [filterCategory, debouncedSearch]);

`setCurrentPage(1)` in this effect runs after render, so when `filterCategory`/`debouncedSearch` change you’ll likely trigger a fetch with the *old* `currentPage` (because `fetchTasks` depends on `filterCategory`/`debouncedSearch`), then immediately trigger a second fetch for page 1. Consider resetting `currentPage` in the filter/search change handlers (or gating the fetch effect) so only one request is made for the intended page.

> @@ -102,12 +113,21 @@ const TasksScreen = () => {
   const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
   const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
   const [actionLoading, setActionLoading] = useState(false);
+  const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(1);
+  const [totalCount, setTotalCount] = useState(0);
+  const [showMobileFilters, setShowMobileFilters] = useState(false);
+
+  const totalPages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(totalCount / PAGE_SIZE));
 
   useEffect(() => {

`totalPages` is derived from `totalCount`, but `currentPage` isn’t clamped when `totalCount` shrinks (e.g., deleting the last item on the last page). That can leave `currentPage > totalPages`, render an empty list, and hide pagination controls (`totalPages > 1` becomes false). Consider clamping `currentPage` when `totalCount`/`totalPages` change (or adjusting after delete) so the UI always lands on a valid page.


> +            <>
+              <TaskList tasks={tasks} onTaskSelect={setSelectedTask} selectedTaskId={selectedTask?.id} />
+              {totalPages > 1 && (
+                <div className="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mt-6">
+                  <button
+                    onClick={() => setCurrentPage((p) => p - 1)}
+                    disabled={currentPage === 1}
+                    className="flex items-center gap-1 px-4 py-2 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 text-sm text-gray-600 hover:bg-gray-50 disabled:opacity-40 disabled:cursor-not-allowed transition-colors"

When switching pages, `selectedTask` is kept as-is. This can show details for a task that isn’t in the current page’s list, and actions (complete/postpone/delete) will operate on that “stale” task. Consider clearing `selectedTask` on page change (or when the selected id is not present in the newly loaded `tasks`).

> @@ -116,19 +136,20 @@ const TasksScreen = () => {
